WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.580
So imagine you're wrapping up a night
out.

00:00:03.583 --> 00:00:06.280
You're ready to head home, but driving isn't an option,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.800
and you don't want to wait for public transit.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:15.180
So you use your phone and you fire up Uber, or Lyft, or whatever ride-hailing app floats your boat.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:21.500
20 minutes later you're faced
with this screen. Do you tip your driver or not?

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.100
No.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.760
Sometimes.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:25.560
...Sometimes.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.060
I can't tell you the last time I tipped an Uber or Lyft.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.630
Sometimes I'll do it. Sometimes I won't.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.080
I should tip.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.240
I tip every single ride.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.980
Wow. Aren't we just like, much better than me.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.800
So riders are all over the place with how they're tipping.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.700
Turns out, only about 35% of riders tip consistently.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.260
When I talk to drivers,
tipping is definitely a big deal for them.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.980
It's something that's on their
minds, and it can make or break a ride for them.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.380
This is Shirin Ghaffary.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.780
I write about tech and labor.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.300
App-based drivers and cab drivers perform a very similar service.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.400
But riders in the US pretty consistently agree:

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.120
cab drivers should be tipped.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.220
I would never walk out of a cab without tipping the driver.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.880
I instinctively tip the cab driver.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.420
So what's going on here?

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.500
Why is there so much confusion

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.240
around whether you should tip on apps like Uber or Lyft?

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.460
This split, between how riders tip cab
drivers versus app-based drivers,

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.260
can be traced back to the origins of Uber:

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.000
the company that made smartphone ride-hailing a thing in the first place.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.300
Uber definitely wasn't the first ride-hailing app, but it was the one that scaled the most quickly,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.560
and became a household name the most quickly.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.620
By 2015, Uber accounted for over 90% of all app-based rides.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.680
Uber set the standard in terms of what's the right driver-rider courtesy.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.760
And for years, Uber set the standard that what's right is not tipping your driver.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:02.120
From the moment Uber entered the market in 2009, there wasn't even an option to tip in the app.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.000
Over the next few years, the company told
curious customers over Twitter that a

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:13.000
20% tip was already built in to the fare
and that there was no need for riders to tip.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.300
Uber also told riders that its
drivers take home the majority of the fare.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.060
I thought that Uber and Lyft were
just like showering their drivers with

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:24.250
money so that you didn't have to tip.
I've always thought that the tip was

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.960
built into the cost.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.020
Uber's messaging around tipping had a huge impact.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.760
Even as other ride-hailing
apps joined the market with tipping

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:37.060
built right into the app,  riders still
saw these services as something that

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.780
didn't require a tip.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.440
Still, for a time,
even though app-based drivers weren't

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.740
making much in tips, their pay was pretty
good and more importantly, stable.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.460
This chart shows what app-based drivers made
between 2012 and 2014.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.000
Even though it goes up and down,
it basically hovers around $1500 a month.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.020
But that didn't last.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.500
As the ride-hailing
market got more crowded, Uber and its

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.340
competitors started to lower their fares
to stay competitive.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.380
There's been many reports that Uber drivers' pay has been going down over the years.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.460
Uber's saying that its commission is 25% but there are
some reports showing that it actually

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.680
may be as high as 40 percent. And in some
cases, they're actually lowering

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.460
the rate that they give drivers per mile.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.000
After 2014, app-based driver's pay dropped dramatically.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:35.540
And by 2018, drivers were making nearly half what they were making just six years before.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.060
You started to hear drivers get more and more disgruntled.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.060
You saw this sort of
community form of drivers who felt like

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.510
these companies may be ripping them off
or not giving them their fair share

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.700
and the lack of tip became a real problem
because the amount of money that Uber

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.480
drivers were getting was comparable to
how much to be getting in a cab

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.840
where most people would tip.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.180
To make matters worse
for drivers, the reasons Uber had given

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.590
for not including tipping in its app,
those turned out to be not entirely true.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:06.510
Uber had said that tipping is included
in our bill. That was sort of debunked

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:10.120
and actually some people ended up suing
Uber over that claim in part

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.100
and they ended up settling.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.880
Finally, drivers'
dissatisfaction reached a turning point.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:28.480
In 2017, Uber's then-CEO Travis Kalanick gets in an Uber and as he's leaving the driver brings up

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.680
driver pay to Travis.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.200
And Travis sort of starts to blame the driver.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.600
Definitely it was an inflection point because it made the company look like it didn't care about its drivers.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:58.620
And I think when you have that hand-in-hand with this no tipping policy, it was not a good look for Uber.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:05.160
Several months after this video,
Uber finally added a tipping option to to its app.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.500
But that wasn't enough to change
the pervasive no-tip culture

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.280
the company had already built.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.640
I always tip cab
drivers but I don't always tip Uber drivers.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:14.700
I didn't know people tipped.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.020
Really? I thought everyone tipped.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.400
So should you tip your Uber driver?

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.560
Well, that's still up to you.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.180
But just keep in mind,

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.460
just like cab drivers, app-based
drivers do not automatically get tipped.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.620
And if you tip in a cab, you should
probably be doing the same for app-based rides.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:40.540
Reconsidering how we tip isn't
going to fix the larger problems

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:44.080
with the ride-hailing industry, but it's still
a good thing to do.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.380
I will say that the gig economy is tough.
It's a tough gig.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.960
So, anything you can do to reward
people for good work, I think, is nice.

